We use **WebSockets when we want continuous, real-time communication between a browser and a server**.

For your kind of project, this is actually very useful.

### Normal HTTP vs WebSocket

**HTTP:**

```text
Browser ──────> Server
        "Any new data?"

Browser <────── Server
        "No"

Browser ──────> Server
        "Any new data?"

Browser <────── Server
        "Temperature = 42"
```

The browser has to keep asking.

With **WebSocket**:

```text
Browser <══════════════════> Server
          ONE OPEN CONNECTION

Server ──────> Temperature = 42
Server ──────> Temperature = 43
Server ──────> Temperature = 44
Server ──────> Temperature = 45
```

The server can **push data whenever it changes**.

---

## Why this matters for your Three.js project

Imagine your setup:

```text
       PHYSICAL WORLD
           
ESP32 → Flow Sensor
  │
  │ Wi-Fi
  ▼
Flask / Python
  │
  │ WebSocket
  ▼
JavaScript
  │
  ▼
Three.js
  │
  ▼
3D Digital Twin
```

Suppose the ESP32 measures:

```text
Flow = 10 L/min
```

Your 3D pipeline might show normal flow.

Then suddenly:

```text
Flow = 2 L/min
```

Flask can immediately send:

```json
{
  "flow": 2,
  "status": "LEAK"
}
```

to the browser through the WebSocket.

Three.js receives it and could immediately:

```text
        3D PIPELINE

████████████████
       ↓
   🔴 LEAK
       ↓
████████████████

Flow: 2 L/min
Status: LEAK
```

No page refresh. No repeated requests.

