# Why not just use HTTP?

You **can** use HTTP.

For example:

```text
ESP32
  ↓
HTTP POST
  ↓
Flask
  ↓
Browser asks every 1 second
  ↓
GET /data
```

This is called **polling**.

For a small project, polling is actually perfectly fine.

But imagine:

```text
100 sensors
10 updates/second
multiple dashboards
real-time 3D visualization
```

Constantly asking:

> "Anything new?"

becomes inefficient.

WebSocket gives you a persistent communication channel.

---

# Simple analogy

### HTTP

📞

You call your friend:

> "Anything new?"

Friend:

> "No."

10 seconds later:

> "Anything new?"

> "No."

10 seconds later:

> "Temperature increased."

😂

### WebSocket

📞 **Call stays open.**

Friend says:

> "Temperature increased."

You immediately hear it.

Then:

> "Pressure dropped."

You immediately hear that too.

---

# WebSocket is NOT a replacement for everything

This is important.

Use normal HTTP when you have something like:

```text
Browser → GET machine information
Browser → POST login
Browser → Upload file
Browser → Request report
```

Use WebSocket when you need:

```text
LIVE SENSOR DATA
LIVE CHAT
LIVE NOTIFICATIONS
LIVE DASHBOARDS
LIVE GAME STATE
REAL-TIME CONTROL
```

For your **ESP32 + Flask + Three.js digital twin**, WebSockets become particularly useful once you want **live sensor updates and live machine-state animation**.

